  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 112 PM CST Tue Nov 24 2026\n\nREFS indicates the upper level low and its trough will continue to \ntrack in from the north/northwest where it will track over eastern \nSD/MN through tonight and forecast to be over northeastern  NE/IA/SD \nborder by 12Z. This low is driven by a strong northwest to southeast \noriented jet streak on the backside of the system. Additional \nsupport from a weak LLJ and an increasing saturated profile will \nlead to snow development this afternoon with snow continuing through \nlate tonight/early Wednesday morning across portions of north \ncentral through northeastern SD into west central MN. 18Z RAP \nsoundings at KABR and southeastward to KATY indicate we are dealing \nwith drier air at the lower levels (as we are on the backside of \nthis high). Earlier this morning we have seen very light \nsnow/flurries over portions of north central and NESD. As the low \ninches closer to the CWA and snow starts to fall, it may be very \nlight before evaporating cooling sets in, as soundings indicate the \nwet bulb temp decreasing, and we get a saturated profile at the \nsurface before losing saturation aloft late tonight through early \nWednesday morning. The last of the snow is forecast to exit east \ncentral SD by 12Z or a bit before.\n\nREFS probability of snowfall of a half inch or more has increased a \nbit ranging from 60-90% over portions of north central SD (mainly the \nLeola Hills and portions of northeastern SD (Watertown and \nsouthward). NBM is lower (and has been for the past several days) \nwith this probability only 25-50%. Looking at the probability of an \ninch or more has increased with REFs at 50-75% Watertown and \nsouthward/Leola Hills while NBM is 20% or less. Deterministic models \nsuch as the EC, GFS, HRRR do indicate the potential for an inch or \nmore over east central SD through southeastern SD/MN but exact \nlocation varies due to the low strengthening/WAA setup. So confidence \nis high for the location of the overall snow but lower on exact \namounts (mainly 0.5-1\"). Most of the snow accumulation will be on \ngrassy surfaces and cars but roads may be slick at times during the \nevening commute and morning commute on Wednesday. Behind the \ndeparting system, REFS indicates fog potential developing over north \ncentral SD late this evening, with patchy to areas of fog filtering \nsouthward across central SD through Wednesday morning. \n\nOtherwise, dry conditions expected for the rest of Wednesday through \nFriday for holiday travel. A shortwave, and its surface low, will \ntrack west to east across the Northern Plains into Canada Friday \nwith the region on the backside of this trough by Saturday morning. \nAs of now, no precip is forecast with the passing of the surface low \n(which will stay to our north). With the axis of the surface trough \npassing, winds will switch (and increase) from south to more \nwesterly then northwesterly through Saturday. The highest gusts are \nforecast across central SD with a probability of wind gusts>45mph is \nonly at 15-30% west of the Mo River.\n\nOvernight lows will range in the 20s tonight and in the mid to upper \n30s for Wednesday with temperatures on a warming trend for the end \nof the week with highs forecast in the 40s to low 50s on Friday! Per \nwinds and ongoing dry fuels, there is elevated fire weather \nconcerns across central SD.",
